willing to go far in order to get  it the thing about the question about  hungary is the fact that they are at  dangerously low levels of gas reserves  and so they feel the need to go out of  their way to secure the gas supply for  their country i mean  on this in the same way poland for  example is in a really good position has  secured 90 percent or more of what they  actually need and so question the  european unions or the european  commission's decision to cut down energy  gas consumption by 15 because they think  we need that to kind of keep our  businesses moving so i think when you  see that there's such a big disconnect  um within the eu you can kind of see  cracks of disagreement i think it's been  around for a while it's becoming obvious  now and i think we can see that member  states are starting to go their own way  kind of make their own deals i think  that's going to be interesting to see  how the eu actually can pull this off  yeah i imagine to some degree that this  is a bit of what uh putin was counting  on that that these cracks would would  widen as the months go on but can you  tell me even with all that in mind  is it realistic or how realistic  realistic is it that they could pull off  a 15 cut across the board well so the 15  cut is a proposal right now all the  member states have to agree to it  they're in conversation today we should  hear more about a decision next week  but realistically i think that i think  it's ambitious i think it's possible but  i also think that we must keep in mind  the current decisions the current  promises challenges kept by the german  government to phase out coal soon by  2030 they want to cut their greenhouse  gas emissions by 55  for that you got to be a bit more  careful about you know they've restarted  coal plants again so i i do hope that  it's possible to cut that dependence but  i would like to see what are the costs  or benefits for that sure yes what i'm  understanding from your point there is  that the eu has these commitments to get  off of fossil fuels that they um are in  some cases neglecting and so it remains  to be seen how much they can follow  through and that brings us very well to  our next point and a bit to coming back  where we started um all of this we were  talking about intense heat  here in europe  and i want to talk about where this  looming energy crisis that we've been  speaking about also comes together with  the global climate crisis  now the petersburg climate dialogue took  place in berlin this week germany's  foreign minister annalena belbach called  on the global community to step up its  efforts and take immediate action  against man-made global warming she  called it the biggest security problem  facing everyone on this earth here's  more from her  eight years left  to almost half global emission  that's the commitment we made in glasgow  but we also have to focus much more on  the climate impacts that we have failed  to avert  we owe this to the people around the  world  in the pacific islands in sahel  and in other regions who are already  suffering  and who already feel the impacts of the  current  climate crisis  well as a reminder there that the  effects of climate change are nothing  new to many parts of the globe even if  they're only just starting to bite here  in europe so i want to take the next  question back to vazili i'm hoping that  you can maybe tell me about russia's  stance on the green energy transition  obviously a country very reliant on  fossil fuels  when it comes to its economy does  breaking free from fossil fuels pose an  existential threat to russia  well russia is a bit different from  europe because uh for a long time um  green transition climate crisis were not  really recognized as existential threats  to russia it changed um over the past  few years basically once uh the  permafrost in the russian arctic region  started melting and there were lots of  problems with  you know pipes uh falling apart and so  on there were environmental disasters  this made the russian government change  its mind and now there is a strategy  a climate friendly strategy in russia  but of course it is by far not as  ambitious as the  environmental plans as the climate  policies of the eu now what uh what do  the  uh eu policies climate policies mean for  russia um of course uh if  really the eu manages to use more  renewable energy to use less  fossil fuels  a large part of which comes from russia  this is not good news for  for russia but it is not dramatic as  long as russia can reorient its  exports of oil and gas to other markets  to china to india  to other non-western countries  it is  it is reasonably easy to do in the case  of oil this is already being done so for  instance the  deliveries of russian oil to india  already  jumped 50 times  starting from a low basis for instance  with gas it is more problematic because  this requires appropriate infrastructure  at the moment  most russian pipelines gas pipelines run  westwards so around towards europe so  reorienting this gas flows to asia will  be much more difficult it will take a  lot of time but in the long run uh  probably it is not dramatic for russia  as long as other parts of the world are  lagging behind in the implementation of  green agenda behind the eu  well i want to talk about in the long  run here for a second i mean there's so  many issues that we're facing right now  that need to be solved as quickly as  possible it can be easy to only look at  things in the short term but looking  longer term 10 years 20 years longer if  you like  what would it mean for russia if europe  gets off of russian energy or if they  work with these other partners china  india as you're saying also especially  considering that there is global  pressure to move away from fossil fuels  which you just spoke about to some  degree  well here yeah uh here the the thing is  that on the one hand russia will  probably be able to reorient the large  part of it of its energy exports to  non  non-european markets china and so on  that is one thing  another thing is that this reliance on  the energy sector is a problem in itself  so as long as the energy sector  remains the backbone of the russian  economy  it is a problem for the development for  for the russian economic development for  its ambition to  to sustain catching up with the rich  economies that would mean that the  russian economy would  have to grow faster than the global  average and  in the current situation it looks like  the opposite will be the case so that  means that on a global scale the russian  economy will become  an even an ever smaller part of the  global economy so i think this is a  problem uh for russia that is a  structural problem this is nothing new  this excessive reliance reliance on the  energy sector how to solve this problem  you need to invest  and this investment needs to be um  needs to bring new technologies it needs  to  it needs to bring modernization of the  economy i think the current geopolitical  climate it will be more difficult for  russia and this uh  this increased  trade and cooperation with china for  instance will be able to have set the  disruption of economic ties with the  west only to some degree so it in the  long run  the prospects for russia are not very  good i would say  all right well just to round things off  here i want to go with a one final short  question to vada bringing it back here  to the heat we've been facing  in here in germany are people more  concerned about uh the hot temperatures  or are they more concerned about heating  their apartments this winter what's the  feeling on the ground everything they're  concerned about the heat they're  concerned about the heating they will  need in the future they're concerned  about everything and personally i feel  like this may give birth to more  grassroots movements for climate  literacy and i think that may be  important people may finally start to  change their lives for the  better all right well we'll have to  leave it there for now uh that's all we  have for you this week from our little  business desk but we'll be back with  another business special for you next  week i want to thank my guests vasily  astrov and vada imran for joining me  today thank you both  now for your viewers if you'd like more  please hit the like and subscribe button  i'd also encourage you to check out the  dw business series here on youtube  business beyond which takes a look at  business business phenomena shaping the  globe for me and the whole business team  here in berlin thank you so much for  watching until next time take care  you
 

